<body>
<h1>Unit 18: Dispersion</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Waves &amp; Optics</p><p class="summary">Dispersion is the wavelength dependence of refractive index. It explains rainbows and chromatic aberration, it is the reason a spectrum carries chemical information, and it contains one of the sharpest traps in optics: the difference between phase velocity and the speed that carries information.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Dispersion means n depends on wavelength, so different colours bend by different amounts and a lens focuses them at different distances.</li><li>Cauchy's empirical form n = A + B/lambda^2 gives an approximate wavelength dependence in transparent regions.</li><li>Normal dispersion - n rising as lambda falls - is what produces the familiar spectrum order from red to violet.</li><li>Anomalous dispersion near an absorption band reverses the sign of dn/dlambda, and a dispersive prism can then select a single narrow band from white light.</li><li>The group velocity v_g = c/(n - lambda dn/dlambda) can exceed c or become negative in anomalously dispersive media without violating relativity, because the phase velocity carries no information.</li><li>A spectrum is a fingerprint: the blackbody shape gives temperature, the line positions give composition, and the Doppler shift gives motion.</li><li>Glass has a larger refractive index for blue light than for red, so short wavelengths are bent more and a prism sends violet towards the inside of the spectrum.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Cauchy's two-parameter fit is valid only for normal dispersion in transparent regions; it diverges near a resonance and cannot describe absorption at all.</li><li>Information travels at the group velocity, so superluminal phase velocity is a feature of the mathematics, not a violation of causality.</li><li>At a boundary between media the frequency of light does not change; what changes is the speed and hence the wavelength - a classic examination trap.</li><li>Spectral lines are broadened by temperature, pressure and Doppler motion, and that broadening sets the precision with which a line centre can be measured.</li><li>In a dispersive medium the group velocity differs from the phase velocity, so the speed of light in glass quoted as c/n is a phase speed and not the speed at which a pulse front travels.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 3 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Pure spectrum and dispersive power</li><li>Chromatic and spherical aberration</li><li>Achromatism and its applications</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Dispersion means that different colours travel at different speeds, and therefore the colour we see is the colour of the light itself.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The second part is wrong: the colour we perceive is set by the frequency, which is unchanged when light enters a medium. Only the speed and wavelength change.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> When light enters a medium its frequency is fixed by the source, so v = c/n and the wavelength shortens: lambda_medium = lambda_vacuum / n. A prism does not change the frequency of any component, so the spectrum it produces still has the same colours as the source; the prism only separates the directions of the different frequencies.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The dispersive power of a glass is the difference between its refractive indices for violet and red light.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> That difference is a measure of how much the glass disperses, but it is not the dimensionless dispersive power, which is normalised so that different glasses can be compared.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> omega = (n_v - n_r) / (n_y - 1). With n_v = 1.530, n_r = 1.514, n_y = 1.517 the difference is 0.016 but the dispersive power is 0.016 / 0.517 = 0.031. The normalisation matters because glasses differ in their overall index as well as in their dispersion.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The speed of light in a medium can be greater than c, which shows that Einstein is wrong.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The speed that can exceed c is the group velocity of a strongly dispersive pulse, and the group velocity is not the speed at which information or a single crest travels. No signal outruns c.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> v_g = c / (n - lambda dn/dlambda) can be negative or greater than c near an absorption band, but the phase velocity c/n and the velocity of the front of a pulse never exceed c, and causality is preserved. The group velocity is a property of a pulse in a strongly dispersive medium, not a transport speed for anything.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A crown glass has refractive indices n_v = 1.530, n_y = 1.517 and n_r = 1.514. Find its dispersive power, and find the angle of deviation of a 60 degree prism of this glass at minimum deviation for the yellow line.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Dispersive power omega = (n_v - n_r)/(n_y - 1) = (1.530 - 1.514)/(1.517 - 1) = 0.016/0.517 = 0.0310. For the yellow line, n = sin((A + delta_m)/2)/sin(A/2) gives 1.517 = sin((60 + delta_m)/2)/0.5, so sin((60 + delta_m)/2) = 0.7585 and (60 + delta_m)/2 = 49.3 degrees, hence delta_m = 38.6 degrees. The angular dispersion between the violet and red lines is then (n_v - n_r) A = 0.016 x 60 degrees = 0.96 degrees, which matches the typical figure of about 1 degree for a 60 degree crown-glass prism.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the angular dispersion produced by a thin prism between two wavelengths, and relate it to the dispersive power of the glass.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> For a thin prism the deviation for a wavelength lambda is delta = (n(lambda) - 1) A. The angular dispersion between two wavelengths is therefore delta_v - delta_r = (n_v - n_r) A. The dispersive power is defined as omega = (n_v - n_r)/(n_y - 1), so the angular dispersion can be written delta_v - delta_r = omega (n_y - 1) A, which shows that for a fixed glass and apex angle the dispersion is proportional to omega. Since the deviation of a prism is small, a prism is used as the dispersing element of a spectrometer and a telescope is used to view the spectrum without further deviation.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A spectroscope uses a collimator, a prism and a telescope. Explain the function of each part, and explain how the same arrangement can be used to measure the wavelength of a spectral line.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The collimator is a lens with a narrow slit at its focal plane, so the light from the slit emerges as a parallel beam; without it the prism would simply produce a blurred image of the source. The prism disperses the beam by an amount that depends on wavelength, so the emergent beam contains a spectrum. The telescope then focuses that spectrum onto the eyepiece, and the scale on the prism table or the vernier measures the deviation angle for each colour. Because the deviation of a prism in the minimum-deviation position is known as a function of n, and n as a function of wavelength, measuring the deviation of an unknown line and inverting the relation yields its wavelength. This is how stellar spectra give the composition of stars and how the redshift of a galaxy is measured.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>dn/dlambda for crown glass (visible)</td><td>about -5e-5 per nm</td></tr><tr><td>Visible spectrum</td><td>about 380 nm to 750 nm</td></tr><tr><td>Speed of light in crown glass (n = 1.52)</td><td>about 1.97e8 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Angular dispersion of a 60 degree crown-glass prism</td><td>about 1.0 degree</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can a dispersion profile be engineered that resolves wavelengths beyond the diffraction limit of the device itself?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Metasurface and metamaterial designs are actively pursuing this; causality and usable bandwidth are the limiting constraints.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How are atomic energy levels and their linewidths set, and how far can they be predicted from first principles?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Spectroscopy is precise enough to test atomic theory at parts per billion, which keeps refining rather than contradicting it.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Using Cauchy's formula and a measured index at two wavelengths, predict the order in which colours emerge from a prism.</li><li>Explain why the same prism shows a much narrower spectrum when immersed in water.</li><li>Compare the resolving power of a prism and a diffraction grating for the same beam width, and identify which is better for faint astronomical sources.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/dispersion">Read the chapter notes: Dispersion</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-prism">Prism 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-optics">Optics theory lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/science-overview/science-explainers/spectroscopy-101-types-of-spectra-and-spectroscopy/">NASA: Spectroscopy 101</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-4-diffraction-gratings">OpenStax: Diffraction Gratings</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-5-circular-apertures-and-resolution">OpenStax: Circular Apertures and Resolution</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
